WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:09.762
This is Chapter 3: writing
a validation kit check.

4
00:00:09.762 --> 00:00:13.787
A check is only evidence if it can't
change what it measures, can't pass

5
00:00:13.787 --> 00:00:18.100
when it failed to look, and asks for no
more access than the question needs.

6
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.752
We'll build one new check end to end,
on soft match and hard match takeover,

7
00:00:22.752 --> 00:00:27.325
and along the way meet a read that
only a Global Administrator can make.

8
00:00:27.325 --> 00:00:31.625
Then the tests, the safety
rules, two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:31.625 --> 00:00:37.140
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:41.878
Why this check?

11
00:00:41.878 --> 00:00:45.737
Gate 3 of the implementation guide
maps attributes through sync,

12
00:00:45.737 --> 00:00:49.882
and before the first user from Active
Directory lands in a tenant that

13
00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:54.241
already has cloud accounts, the sync
engine has to decide who matches whom.

14
00:00:54.241 --> 00:00:56.977
It can join on an email
address or user name,

15
00:00:56.977 --> 00:01:00.965
which is soft match, or on the
source anchor, which is hard match.

16
00:01:00.965 --> 00:01:05.890
Left open, either one lets an on-premises
object take over a cloud account.

17
00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:10.603
Microsoft says keep both blocked except
during an approved matching window.

18
00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:14.040
So the question is simple, which
makes it a good first check.

19
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.870
It also hides a lesson.

20
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.983
Next, which cloud.

21
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:24.958
The kit reads Microsoft Graph, and
the host depends on the tenant.

22
00:01:24.958 --> 00:01:29.781
A GCC tenant sits on the worldwide
endpoints, so it reads the same Graph

23
00:01:29.781 --> 00:01:34.770
host as a commercial tenant; GCC High
and the Defense cloud have their own.

24
00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.355
The sync read this chapter needs
is available in all of them,

25
00:01:38.355 --> 00:01:39.745
at version one point oh.

26
00:01:39.745 --> 00:01:41.935
And the kit holds the line on hosts:

27
00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:46.235
if Graph hands back a next page
that points anywhere other than the

28
00:01:46.235 --> 00:01:50.810
cloud's own Graph host, the kit
refuses it, so the token never leaves.

29
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.340
Every check in the kit
has the same five parts,

30
00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.398
and once you see them, adding a
check is mostly filling in a pattern.

31
00:02:01.398 --> 00:02:05.685
There's a catalog entry that says what
the check is and what access it needs.

32
00:02:05.685 --> 00:02:08.110
A function, named from the check's ID.

33
00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.460
A read through the kit's single data path.

34
00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.098
Rows that turn what was read
into findings and a status.

35
00:02:14.098 --> 00:02:17.532
And fixtures with tests, so
the check has to pass clean

36
00:02:17.532 --> 00:02:20.810
data and fail planted
problems, with an exact count.

37
00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.597
The README's permission table mirrors the
catalog, and a test keeps them in step.

38
00:02:25.597 --> 00:02:27.310
Let's build each part.

39
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.235
Here's the catalog entry,
and the lesson hiding in it.

40
00:02:34.235 --> 00:02:37.936
The read itself is innocent:
get the tenant's sync settings,

41
00:02:37.936 --> 00:02:41.097
with a read-only permission
that sounds harmless.

42
00:02:41.097 --> 00:02:44.548
But Learn lists no application
permission for it at all,

43
00:02:44.548 --> 00:02:49.648
and for a signed-in user it names exactly
one supported role, Global Administrator.

44
00:02:49.648 --> 00:02:51.856
So a check that only reads, run live,

45
00:02:51.856 --> 00:02:55.047
would need the most powerful
role in the tenant.

46
00:02:55.047 --> 00:02:58.385
Being read-only is necessary,
but it isn't enough.

47
00:02:58.385 --> 00:03:01.260
The next slide shows what
the check does about it.

48
00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.235
So the check does three
things differently.

49
00:03:07.235 --> 00:03:10.097
It's optional: it never
runs unless you name it.

50
00:03:10.097 --> 00:03:14.915
It prefers an export: a Global Administrator
who's already signed in for other

51
00:03:14.915 --> 00:03:17.173
reasons saves the response to a file,

52
00:03:17.173 --> 00:03:20.635
and the kit reads the file
without ever needing the role.

53
00:03:20.635 --> 00:03:23.352
And it says so, in plain
words in the catalog,

54
00:03:23.352 --> 00:03:28.398
so a security officer knows what a live
run would demand before anyone tries it.

55
00:03:28.398 --> 00:03:32.769
The program's design guidance follows
from that: never create a standing

56
00:03:32.769 --> 00:03:37.200
Global Administrator identity just
so a check can run on a schedule.

57
00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:42.063
Here's the function.

58
00:03:42.063 --> 00:03:45.859
It reads the export if there is
one, otherwise it asks Graph,

59
00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:50.526
and if the tenant has no sync object at
all, it says the check doesn't apply.

60
00:03:50.526 --> 00:03:52.900
The important part is
the wrapper around it.

61
00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:58.216
Every check runs inside the same body
wrapper, which looks up the catalog entry,

62
00:03:58.216 --> 00:04:02.550
records every read, and turns any
exception into an error result.

63
00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.938
That means a read that throws
can never come back as a pass.

64
00:04:05.938 --> 00:04:10.888
So resist the urge to catch exceptions
inside your check to make it more robust.

65
00:04:10.888 --> 00:04:13.940
That's exactly how a
check passes while blind.

66
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.865
There's exactly one way a check reads
tenant data, and that's on purpose.

67
00:04:21.865 --> 00:04:26.090
In a fixture run, the data path
reads a file from the fixture folder.

68
00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:28.615
Live, it calls Graph with the address.

69
00:04:28.615 --> 00:04:31.278
And an export comes in
through the input reader.

70
00:04:31.278 --> 00:04:35.839
All three are logged the same way, so
when a reviewer opens the evidence,

71
00:04:35.839 --> 00:04:40.165
they can tell from the evidence
alone which path a result came from:

72
00:04:40.165 --> 00:04:44.177
a fixture, a live tenant, or a
file a Global Administrator saved.

73
00:04:44.177 --> 00:04:46.247
That matters for this check above all,

74
00:04:46.247 --> 00:04:50.820
because the path is what tells you
whether the powerful role was ever needed.

75
00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:55.608
Now the rows.

76
00:04:55.608 --> 00:04:58.907
A block that's off is a
finding, and the check fails.

77
00:04:58.907 --> 00:05:01.418
But if there's an
approved matching window,

78
00:05:01.418 --> 00:05:05.220
the same state is a warning: someone
should look, it isn't a failure.

79
00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.062
The window is a date, so
the day after it closes,

80
00:05:08.062 --> 00:05:12.595
the warning turns back into a finding
without anyone editing anything.

81
00:05:12.595 --> 00:05:15.108
Soft match on user name is just context,

82
00:05:15.108 --> 00:05:18.720
since Microsoft says it can't
be turned off once enabled.

83
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.507
And one rule matters more than it
looks: a missing value is read as off.

84
00:05:23.507 --> 00:05:28.080
If the response shape changes, you
get a finding, not a silent pass.

85
00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.118
A check is tested in both directions.

86
00:05:34.118 --> 00:05:37.663
The fixtures are a fictional
tenant in two states:

87
00:05:37.663 --> 00:05:40.517
one with both blocks
on, one with both off.

88
00:05:40.517 --> 00:05:44.824
Each copies the shape of Learn's example
response, with one extra property

89
00:05:44.824 --> 00:05:48.918
the check shouldn't read, so a check
that reads the wrong one gets caught.

90
00:05:48.918 --> 00:05:52.584
Adding the check to the
table-driven test is a single line,

91
00:05:52.584 --> 00:05:56.642
and that line buys four tests,
including an exact failure count.

92
00:05:56.642 --> 00:06:00.318
Then a short block covers what's
particular to this check:

93
00:06:00.318 --> 00:06:03.055
the window, the export,
and the empty tenant.

94
00:06:03.055 --> 00:06:07.380
With the README and catalog
updated, the whole suite passes.

95
00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:15.155
The safety tests are why you can trust
a new check without re-proving it.

96
00:06:15.155 --> 00:06:19.779
They parse every script in the kit and
fail the build on anything that could

97
00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:24.030
change state, and every Graph call has
to say, literally, that it's a read.

98
00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:25.847
There's one pinned exception,

99
00:06:25.847 --> 00:06:29.330
a beta action that evaluates
a rule and changes nothing.

100
00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.029
Because this check reads only
through the shared data path,

101
00:06:33.029 --> 00:06:35.067
it passes those tests untouched.

102
00:06:35.067 --> 00:06:38.918
And the fix for a finding is a write,
so it never goes in the check.

103
00:06:38.918 --> 00:06:43.900
It goes in the change record, run by
the identity team under change control.

104
00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:52.125
This is the chapter's verified-facts
table, the calls the check depends on.

105
00:06:52.125 --> 00:06:54.576
The read needs only a read permission,

106
00:06:54.576 --> 00:06:59.162
but only a Global Administrator can
make it, and only as a signed-in user.

107
00:06:59.162 --> 00:07:02.562
The fix needs the matching write
permission and the same role.

108
00:07:02.562 --> 00:07:06.240
The kit's one exception to
read-only, the rule evaluation,

109
00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:09.838
is still in beta, and that's
labeled wherever it appears.

110
00:07:09.838 --> 00:07:14.882
Underneath it all, the shared reader
does two things every Graph client must:

111
00:07:14.882 --> 00:07:18.725
it follows the next page link
as given until there isn't one,

112
00:07:18.725 --> 00:07:22.050
and when Graph says slow
down, it waits as told.

113
00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:26.850
Who's involved?

114
00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.440
Developers and engineers extend the kit,

115
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.563
and they need PowerShell
and the basics of Pester.

116
00:07:32.563 --> 00:07:34.760
Security officers read the results,

117
00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:38.862
and the catalog tells them up front
what access a live run would take.

118
00:07:38.862 --> 00:07:41.603
A Global Administrator
appears exactly once,

119
00:07:41.603 --> 00:07:45.713
to save the export while they're
already signed in for something else.

120
00:07:45.713 --> 00:07:48.600
Nothing in the kit itself
ever runs with that role.

121
00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.665
And when a finding needs fixing,
the identity team makes the change,

122
00:07:52.665 --> 00:07:55.400
from the change record,
under change control.

123
00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.730
The check only ever reports.

124
00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:06.767
Lab 1 runs on a laptop with PowerShell
7 and Pester; nothing signs in.

125
00:08:06.767 --> 00:08:11.571
First you run the new check against the
failing fixture and see a failure with a

126
00:08:11.571 --> 00:08:14.526
count of two: two findings,
one informational row,

127
00:08:14.526 --> 00:08:16.743
and one read logged from the fixture.

128
00:08:16.743 --> 00:08:19.549
Then you open a matching
window that ends tomorrow,

129
00:08:19.549 --> 00:08:21.354
and the result becomes a warning.

130
00:08:21.354 --> 00:08:25.005
Move the date a day past the
window, and it's a failure again.

131
00:08:25.005 --> 00:08:27.541
Finally you hand it a small export file,

132
00:08:27.541 --> 00:08:32.330
and the evidence shows the result
came from the input, not from Graph.

133
00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.805
The rest of Lab 1 shows the guardrails.

134
00:08:38.805 --> 00:08:42.899
You ask the kit what a live run would
need, and it prints the permission

135
00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:46.705
and names the Global Administrator
role, before anyone signs in.

136
00:08:46.705 --> 00:08:49.517
You confirm the check isn't
part of a default run.

137
00:08:49.517 --> 00:08:51.593
You run the kit's whole test suite.

138
00:08:51.593 --> 00:08:54.595
And then the best step:
you deliberately break the

139
00:08:54.595 --> 00:08:57.379
check so it reads a property
that doesn't exist.

140
00:08:57.379 --> 00:09:01.519
The failing fixture still fails,
but now the passing one fails too,

141
00:09:01.519 --> 00:09:03.818
because a missing value reads as off.

142
00:09:03.818 --> 00:09:06.343
That's the unsafe default doing its job.

143
00:09:06.343 --> 00:09:09.380
Put it back, and you've
seen every outcome.

144
00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:17.405
Lab 2 uses a test tenant that
already syncs from Active Directory.

145
00:09:17.405 --> 00:09:21.449
The export is the only step that
needs a Global Administrator:

146
00:09:21.449 --> 00:09:24.780
they sign in, save the response
to a file, and sign out.

147
00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:29.380
Read that file before you take it;
it's configuration, not personal data.

148
00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:33.162
Then anyone runs the check on
the export, without signing in,

149
00:09:33.162 --> 00:09:36.480
and the evidence says the
result came from the input.

150
00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:38.383
Compare it line by line with the file,

151
00:09:38.383 --> 00:09:42.505
because that's how you catch a response
shape that differs from Learn's example.

152
00:09:42.505 --> 00:09:46.960
Then delete the export; the
run's manifest is the evidence.

153
00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:53.190
Each of these dangers is a way a check can

154
00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:56.197
quietly stop being evidence,
and each has a guard.

155
00:09:56.197 --> 00:10:00.629
The most tempting is creating a standing
Global Administrator so a check

156
00:10:00.629 --> 00:10:04.697
can run on a timer; the guard is an
optional check that reads an export.

157
00:10:04.697 --> 00:10:08.131
The most dangerous is a failed
read that reports a pass;

158
00:10:08.131 --> 00:10:11.184
the wrapper turns every
exception into an error.

159
00:10:11.184 --> 00:10:15.163
Others are subtler: a missing
property read as on, a fixture that

160
00:10:15.163 --> 00:10:19.523
matches the code instead of the
service, a warning that never expires.

161
00:10:19.523 --> 00:10:23.340
Notice that every guard is
built in, not remembered.

162
00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:30.115
Here's what a reviewer checks
before a new check ships.

163
00:10:30.115 --> 00:10:32.528
The name, function and track agree.

164
00:10:32.528 --> 00:10:36.683
The catalog names the least privilege
and the role straight from Learn,

165
00:10:36.683 --> 00:10:40.839
and if that role is privileged, the
check is optional and reads an export.

166
00:10:40.839 --> 00:10:43.707
It reads only through
the shared data path,

167
00:10:43.707 --> 00:10:47.803
hides no failed read, and treats
every missing control as off.

168
00:10:47.803 --> 00:10:51.885
Its fixtures follow Learn's shape,
its tests cover every status,

169
00:10:51.885 --> 00:10:55.890
the README knows about it, and
the safety tests pass untouched.

170
00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:59.215
Next up is Chapter 4,
dynamic rules as code.

171
00:10:59.215 --> 00:11:00.380
Thanks for watching.
